// pfodWeb NOTE: dedicated-hardware pin-exclusion audit, 2026-07-13.
// Board: Raspberry Pi Pico W (RP2040 + Infineon CYW43439 WiFi/BLE chip),
// flagship reference board for the rp2040w chip bucket.
//
// This board's own pins_arduino.h (variants/rp2040/rp2040w/rpipicow/
// pins_arduino.h) is ALREADY bespoke and self-contained -- it does NOT
// `#include "../generic/common.h"` the way sparkfun_thingplusrp2350 does,
// and its own "pfodWeb NOTE" header comment already explains why: GPIO23/
// 24/25/29 are wired internally to the CYW43439 WiFi/BLE chip and are
// deliberately NOT declared as D<n>/A<n> aliases, and PIN_LED (64u) is
// documented as "not parsed as a pin here" because it addresses the
// WiFi chip's own GPIO0, not a real RP2040 pin. No board.json existed
// before this audit; this file makes that already-correct pin exclusion
// an explicit, reviewable part of the per-board config (matching the
// audit convention used for every other board in this family) and
// records the research trail confirming it.
//
// Sources consulted:
//  - This board's own pins_arduino.h (variants/rp2040/rp2040w/rpipicow/
//    pins_arduino.h): self-contained, includes <cyw43_wrappers.h>,
//    `#define PIN_LED (64u)`, declares D0-D22/D26-D28/A0-A2/SS/MOSI/
//    MISO/SCK/SDA/SCL aliases but NO alias at all for GPIO23/24/25/29
//    or for pin 64 -- confirmed by direct read that no `static const
//    uint8_t X = ...` line in the file resolves to 23, 24, 25, 29, or
//    64. This matters mechanically: build_boards.js's buildEsp32Board()
//    (used for the "rp2040" family) unions pins_arduino.h aliases with
//    any chipGpios keys to decide which GPIOs appear as pins at all
//    (see pfodWeb_src/build_boards.js lines ~531-539); a GPIO with no
//    alias and no chipGpios entry simply never appears in the output.
//    So these 4 WiFi-chip pins and the pseudo-pin 64 are ALREADY fully
//    absent from the generated board -- adding chipGpios entries for
//    them would be actively wrong, since chipGpios keys are unioned
//    BACK into the exposed-GPIO set (would wrongly re-introduce pins
//    that aren't even physically broken out on this board).
//  - variants/rp2040/rp2040w/board.json (chip bucket): its own comment
//    block already documents "rpipicow/rpipico2w have bespoke, self-
//    contained pins_arduino.h that exclude the WiFi-chip-reserved GPIOs
//    (23/24/25/29) directly" -- confirms this is the intended, already-
//    implemented design for this specific board.
//  - variants/rp2040/rp2040w/sparkfun_thingplusrp2350/board.json +
//    boardsDetails/rp2040/rp2040w/sparkfun_thingplusrp2350/notes.txt
//    (sibling WiFi-bucket board audited earlier this session): precedent
//    for this file's schema/style, and its own research trail treats
//    GPIO23/24/25/29 exclusion via common.h as "replicating the
//    validated Pico W reference wiring" -- i.e. rpipicow is the
//    canonical source of that reservation this whole chip bucket
//    inherits from.
//  - Official Raspberry Pi Pico W datasheet (raspberrypi.com / pip.
//    raspberrypi.com, RP-008312-DS-pico-w-datasheet.pdf) and multiple
//    independent secondary sources (randomnerdtutorials.com Pico W
//    GPIO pinout guide; Raspberry Pi forum threads "Understanding Pico
//    W shared GPIO with WiFi chip" and "More GPIO - how about 23, 24,
//    25, 29?"): confirm GPIO23/24/25/29 are NOT exposed on the Pico W's
//    pin header at all -- they are the internal SPI-style bus to the
//    CYW43439 radio (GPIO23 = WL wireless enable/SMPS control, GPIO24 =
//    WL data/IRQ line, GPIO25 = WL chip-select, GPIO29 = WL clock line,
//    GPIO29 doubling as ADC3/VSYS-sense only when the WL bus is idle).
//    This differs from the plain (non-W) Pico, where these same GPIO
//    numbers are ordinary user pins (GPIO25 = onboard LED, GPIO29 =
//    ADC3/VSYS, etc.) -- the Pico W's WiFi chip genuinely repurposes
//    them, they are not merely "not recommended".
//  - arduino-pico core source (github.com/earlephilhower/arduino-pico,
//    variants/rpipicow/pins_arduino.h, fetched directly): confirms this
//    repo's copy is a faithful match of the real upstream file --
//    `#define PIN_LED (64u)`, GPIO23/24/25/29 absent from the alias
//    list, `#include <cyw43_wrappers.h>`. This IS the actual board-
//    support source the generated sketches compile against.
//  - Community discussion on the arduino-pico LED_BUILTIN mechanism
//    (Raspberry Pi forums "Pico W onboard LED not working [SOLVED]";
//    github.com/earlephilhower/arduino-pico issue #3297 re: Waveshare
//    RP2350B Plus W LED1/CYW43 GPIO0 offset-64 convention): confirms
//    the onboard LED on Pico W is wired to the CYW43439's own WL_GPIO0
//    pin, not any RP2040 silicon pin. arduino-pico's digitalWrite()/
//    digitalRead() special-case any pin number >=64 by subtracting 64
//    and routing through cyw43_arch_gpio_put()/cyw43_arch_gpio_get(),
//    which only work correctly AFTER cyw43_arch_init() has run (this
//    happens implicitly inside WiFi.begin()/WiFi.mode()).
//  - variants/rp2040/tcp.ino / http.ino (this chip bucket's own
//    templates): confirm setup() calls WiFi.mode(WIFI_STA) then
//    WiFi.begin(...) before any user "extra setup code" runs, so
//    cyw43_arch_init() has always completed by the time a TCP/HTTP
//    sketch's own code could touch pin 64.
//  - variants/serial.ino (this repo's shared Serial-connection
//    template, used by every family including this "serial"-capable
//    WiFi bucket): confirms setup() only calls Serial.begin() and the
//    pfodParser bootstrap -- it never calls WiFi.mode()/WiFi.begin()/
//    cyw43_arch_init(). A Serial-only sketch generated for this board
//    would therefore call digitalWrite(64, ...) (or a codeName built
//    from a bare "64") on an UNINITIALIZED cyw43 bus if pin 64 were
//    tagged as a normal led_high/led_low capability -- a real
//    correctness hazard, not just a style concern.
//
// GPIO23/24/25/29 (CYW43439 wireless bus -- enable/data/CS/clock):
// CONFIRMED not present on the Pico W's pin header at all, internal to
// the WiFi/BLE radio. Already fully excluded by pins_arduino.h having
// no D<n>/A<n> alias for any of them -- NO chipGpios entry added here
// (adding one would incorrectly re-introduce a pin that isn't even
// physically accessible). Matches this repo's rp2040w/board.json chip-
// bucket comment describing this as the correct, already-implemented
// design for this board.
//
// LED (WL_GPIO0 via CYW43439, addressed in arduino-pico as pin 64 /
// PIN_LED): CONFIRMED wired to the wireless chip, not a real RP2040
// GPIO -- not representable as a normal digital_output/led_high/
// led_low capability for two independent reasons: (1) it is outside
// the chip's real GPIO0-29 address space, so it is not "a pin" in the
// sense this Designer's pin picker models; (2) driving it correctly
// requires cyw43_arch_init() to have already run, which this repo's
// shared serial.ino template does NOT do, so a naive digitalWrite(64,
// ...) generated for a Serial-connection sketch on this board would
// hit an uninitialized wireless bus. Left completely untagged (no
// chipGpios entry, matching pins_arduino.h's own "not parsed as a pin
// here" note) rather than guessing a capability that could silently
// generate broken code -- same "no legitimate GPIO-capability alias
// worth surfacing" precedent already used for adafruit_trinkeyrp2040qt
// and wiznet_6300_evb_pico (see sparkfun_thingplusrp2350/notes.txt).
//
// BOOTSEL button: present on Pico W like every RP2040/RP2350 board, but
// read via the boot ROM (not a normal digitalRead()-capable GPIO) --
// no "button" capability tag applies, matching this repo's existing
// convention of never tagging Pico-family BOOTSEL buttons (see
// sparkfun_thingplusrp2350/notes.txt).
//
// No SPI0/Wire0/Serial1/Serial2 header pins needed any override -- they
// are ordinary general-purpose header pins on this board (no onboard
// SD/PSRAM/sensor hardware competes for them the way it does on
// sparkfun_thingplusrp2350), and their spi_*/i2c_*/serial_* bus-role
// tags are already attached automatically by build_boards.js's
// unconditional SDA/SCL/SS/MOSI/MISO/SCK/TX/RX alias matching.
//
// Net result: chipGpios is empty ({}), same as the rp2040w chip-level
// default -- this file exists to make the CYW43-pin and LED reasoning
// an explicit, sourced, per-board record (this audit's standard
// deliverable) rather than to change generated behaviour, which was
// already correct.
//
// See boardsDetails/rp2040/rp2040w/rpipicow/notes.txt for the summary.
{
  "boardName": "RP2040W",
  "displayName": "Raspberry Pi Pico W / 2W",
  "family": "rp2040",
  "inputOnlyGpios": [],
  "connections": ["serial", "tcp", "http", "ble"],
  "pinNotes": {},
  "chipGpios": {},
  "pwm": { "min": 0, "max": 255, "method": "analogWrite" },
  "dac": {},
  "adc": { "bits": 12, "max": 4095, "defaultRefVolts": "3.3" }
}
